<h1>What actually happens inside gVisor when your app makes a syscall</h1>

<div class="col root"><div class="card red"><div class="t">UNTRUSTED USER SPACE</div><div class="d">App process (e.g. Node.js)</div></div>
<div class="arrow" style="flex:0 0 auto">↓ <span class="lbl">fires a syscall (open, socket, read...)</span></div>
<div class="card"><div class="t">INTERCEPTION</div><div class="d">Systrap stub (per-CPU) or KVM platform traps the instruction here, before the host kernel ever sees it</div></div>
<div class="card"><div class="t">TRUSTED USER SPACE - the gVisor sandbox</div></div>
<div class="row"><div class="card blue"><div class="t">Sentry - the Go userspace kernel</div><div class="d">emulates 300+ Linux syscalls in memory</div>
<div class="d">Netstack (embedded, not a process)</div>
<div class="d">full Go TCP/IP stack - builds L2/L3 frames in-process</div></div>
<div class="arrow" style="flex:0 0 auto">→<span class="lbl">shared-mem ring (LISFS)</span></div>
<div class="card blue"><div class="t">Gofer</div><div class="d">filesystem proxy, one per container</div>
<div class="d">Sentry never touches the real host FS directly</div></div>
<div class="card"><div class="t">Both are seccomp-bpf caged:</div><div class="d">Sentry: only network + memory syscalls reach the real host.</div>
<div class="d">Gofer: only file read/write on handles it already validated.</div></div></div>
<div class="card"><div class="t">PRIVILEGED SYSTEM SPACE</div><div class="d">Host Linux Kernel</div>
<div class="d">the application process never speaks to this box directly - every arrow above is the redirection that guarantees it</div></div></div>
<div class="flowstrip"><div>INTERCEPTION &#8594; Sentry - the Go userspace kernel</div>
<div>Sentry - the Go userspace kernel &#8594; restricted host syscalls &#8594; PRIVILEGED SYSTEM SPACE</div>
<div>Gofer &#8594; restricted host syscalls &#8594; PRIVILEGED SYSTEM SPACE</div></div>

</body>
